{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git show bbd7564:docs/02-product-spec.md | nl -ba | sed -n '86,255p'\ngit show bbd7564:docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md | nl -ba | sed -n '105,149p;169,207p'\"","aggregated_output":"    86\t## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\n    87\t\n    88\tEvery criterion maps to at least one test in the acceptance matrix kept in the repository (criterion → test → chart types it applies to → result). \"Property test\" means the statement is checked over at least 1,000 generated datasets. Expected values come from the fixture data and this specification, never from the implementation's output.\n    89\t\n    90\t### 3.1 Faithful representation\n    91\t\n    92\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n    93\t|---|---|---|---|\n    94\t| F1 | Bar and area value axes always include zero. An author `domain` that excludes zero is widened and raises AX110. | Property test on domains, including all-negative and mixed-sign data; `bars` fixture (values 82–97) starts at 0 | R1 |\n    95\t| F2 | Missing values are never drawn as zero and never bridged unless the author asks. An explicit `null` is a gap, silently. An isolated value between two gaps is a visible point. A missing bar shows a \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar. On a time or date axis with a regular step, an absent step is a gap and raises AX105; `missing: 'zero'` on the field turns absent and null values into zeros for counts. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". | `trend` with nulls and `trend` with the same 7 rows deleted render the same gap; `dirty` fixtures; property test that no segment connects across a missing index | R5 |\n    96\t| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\n    97\t| F4 | Three temporal types, never mixed silently. **Calendar dates** (`2026-02-24`, `2026-02`, `2026`) read the same in every viewer time zone. **Instants** (epoch milliseconds declared `type: 'time'`, `Date` objects, ISO strings with an offset) are shown in `timeZone` (default: the viewer's), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the description. **Wall-clock times** (date-time strings without an offset) are shown as written, with hint AX112. Instants that all fall on the same time of day, whole days apart, raise hint AX111 (\"these look like calendar dates\"). A bare number is never assumed to be an instant. | Four fixture variants in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata: (a) date strings: identical text in all three; (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch ms with `type: 'time'`, (d) `Date` objects: each zone's correct local text, zone visible, AX111 raised; with `timeZone: 'UTC'` or `type: 'date'`: identical. Plus: both DST transitions, a leap day, an unparseable date | R3 |\n    98\t| F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A break between two runs is always kept, even when both fall in one pixel column; a slot a series merely has no row for (weekly data beside daily) does not break its line. Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\n    99\t| F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, small decimals, percentages, zoned times) in `en-US`, `de-DE` and `en-IN` | R4 |\n   100\t| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. This rule is for value axes: the y axis of line, area and bar, and both axes of scatter. A continuous x axis of line, area or bar spans exactly its data (plus half a band for bars), so a time series is not padded to the next tick. The automatic domain contains all data, the reference lines and bands the chart draws (a compact chart draws none, so `reference` has no effect there and is reported as an option that does not apply) and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme. A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\n   101\t| F8 | Donut: slices keep the given order, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. The legend shows every slice's value and percentage as text, in slice order. A zero slice is listed without an arc; a `null` slice is listed as \"No data\". A negative value is AX120 and the chart shows its error state. With more than eight slices that have an arc (a positive value), the seven largest are drawn and the rest of those are grouped as \"Other (n)\", with AX121; zero and `null` slices never count toward the limit and are never grouped, so each stays listed on its own; the table lists every category. Shares are shown with at most one fraction digit. An all-zero donut shows the empty state. | `parts` fixture; one test per case | R7, landscape scoreboard 3.1 |\n   102\t| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker (for bars, full and compact alike: color and fill pattern; for every chart type at least 32 distinct combinations, after which combinations repeat), and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\n   103\t| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. The readout's total is the sum when every component has the same sign; when signs are mixed it gives the positive total and the negative total, the two ends the reader sees, not their net. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\n   104\t| F11 | Line, area and scatter are drawn in x order whatever the input order. On line, area and bar, two rows with the same x in one series are AX108 and that series is not drawn. On scatter, points sharing an x (or both coordinates) are ordinary data. | Shuffled `trend` renders identically; duplicate fixture | R5 |\n   105\t| F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\"Values not shown: 2. See table.\", worded so it reads correctly for any count) inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\n   106\t\n   107\t### 3.2 Legibility\n   108\t\n   109\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n   110\t|---|---|---|---|\n   111\t| L1 | No visible text overlaps other text or is clipped, and no label disappears without the reader being told. Remedies, in order: thin (time, date, numeric and `ordered` category axes only); wrap to at most three lines; rotate up to 45°; truncate in the middle, keeping every label distinct, with the full text in tooltip and table. If labels still do not fit, a visible note (\"Some labels hidden. See table.\") appears and AX143 suggests horizontal bars. Axylo never changes a chart's orientation on its own. | In real browsers, after fonts load: for widths 240–1,920 px and generated label sets, zero intersecting text boxes (1 px tolerance), zero text outside the figure, truncated labels pairwise distinct. Repeated with the WCAG 1.4.12 text-spacing overrides, 200% text-only zoom, and a font that loads late | R2 |\n   112\t| L2 | Text is at least 12 CSS px and at least 4.5:1 against what is behind it, including text drawn inside a mark and legend entries for hidden series. Marks, axis lines and focus indicators are at least 3:1. Gridlines are exempt: they are not needed to read a value while the tooltip and table exist. Holds in light and dark. | Computed-style and rendered-pixel checks in both schemes | R7, R10 |\n   113\t| L3 | The chart follows its container with no call from the page, in both directions. With no definite height, the plot is 16:9, clamped between 160 and 480 CSS px tall, and the title, controls and table flow above and below it. A container that starts hidden or zero-sized draws when it gets a size. | Resize 720 → 360 → 720 px; hidden-then-shown test; `height: auto` container is stable over 10 frames | R16, D1 |\n   114\t| L4 | Light or dark is taken from the chart element's computed `color-scheme`; if that is not set, from the luminance of the inherited text color. `--axylo-background` overrides both. Marks repaint within one frame when the scheme, forced colors, contrast preference, or the `class`, `style` or `data-theme` of `<html>` or `<body>` changes. Forced-colors mode uses system colors. | A page that sets `color-scheme`; a class-toggled dark theme without it; a light-only page on a dark device (must stay light); a runtime toggle; forced-colors emulation | R10 |\n   115\t| L5 | A marker on every point appears only when adjacent points are at least 12 px apart (provisional value), and always for isolated points. | `trend` at 720 px has none; a 12-point line has them | R8 |\n   116\t| L6 | Title, subtitle and axis titles with units are part of the chart. A value axis is titled with its label (the field name when none is given) and unit. An x axis that is a measure (number) is titled the same way; a time, date or category x axis is titled only when the author gives a `label`, since its ticks already say what it is. A reference line or band shows its label next to it. Axis titles never overlap tick labels. | Layout tests; `trend` fixture | R9 |\n   117\t| L7 | Empty data shows a message inside the plot area; so do loading, error and Axylo's own internal failures. No invented axis ranges, no blank box. A change of state is announced once, politely. | `empty` fixture: message visible and in the accessibility tree, no tick labels; forced internal error shows the error state | R6 |\n   118\t| L8 | When the figure cannot hold a readable plot (less than 120×80 CSS px for the plot), it shows the title, a short summary (the first sentence of the generated summary, on at most two lines, cut with an ellipsis beyond that; the full summary remains the figure's description) and the table control instead, and raises AX142. | Narrow and short container tests | R2 |\n   119\t\n   120\t### 3.3 Interaction\n   121\t\n   122\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n   123\t|---|---|---|---|\n   124\t| I1 | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the picking rule for the chart type (section 3.6), within one frame at the 95th percentile. On line, area and bar the tooltip lists every visible series at the nearest x; a series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\", distinct from \"No data\". The tooltip stays inside the viewport, does not cover the active mark or any control, can itself be hovered, and lists at most eight rows plus \"+N more\". In a plot too small for it (decision 0004: narrower than 240 px or shorter than 140 px) the same content appears as text in a strip reserved under the plot; where even the strip would leave the plot under the minimum of L8, pointing highlights the datum without a readout and the values are in the keyboard reading and the table. Escape closes it until the pointer reaches a different datum. | Hover at 20 positions including all four edges; sparse-series fixture; narrow tile with eight series; Escape test | R15 |\n   125\t| I2 | Legend entries are toggle buttons with a pressed state, operable by click, Enter and Space. | Keyboard and accessibility-tree tests | R12 |\n   126\t| I3 | Zoom on time, date and numeric x axes is reachable four ways: visible Zoom in, Zoom out and Reset buttons that work with single clicks; drag to select a range; `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift+arrows; and pinch when `zoom: 'touch'` is set. The wheel zooms only while the plot has focus, and never when Ctrl or Meta is held, so browser zoom and page scrolling are not intercepted. | Reach a given range by clicks only; by keyboard only; by drag; page scrolls normally with the pointer over an unfocused chart | D7, R12, WCAG 2.5.1, 2.5.7, 1.4.4 |\n   127\t| I4 | Every interaction works with a single pointer without dragging, and by touch. Every control is at least 24×24 CSS px. A one-finger drag on a chart scrolls the page. | Touch emulation; size checks | WCAG 2.5.7, 2.5.8 |\n   128\t| I5 | First render is not animated. An update may transition for at most 200 ms. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing transitions, on first render or on update. | After `document.fonts.ready`: first frame equals the frame at 2.5 s within a 0.1% pixel tolerance, in both settings; an update under reduced motion reaches its final frame in one step; a 24-chart filter change settles within 200 ms | R14 |\n   129\t| I6 | A chart fed by `append` shows a Pause/Resume control. While paused, new rows are buffered and the view does not move. | Stream test | WCAG 2.2.2 |\n   130\t\n   131\t### 3.4 Accessibility\n   132\t\n   133\tTarget: every WCAG 2.2 level AA success criterion that applies to what Axylo renders, as component-level evidence. Conformance of a page is a property of the page and remains the author's.\n   134\t\n   135\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n   136\t|---|---|---|---|\n   137\t| A1 | Every chart has an accessible name: its `title`, or the heading named by `labelledBy`, or else a generated name (\"Line chart of Sessions by Day\") with AX140 raised in every build. The title's heading level is the author's (`level`), and it can be hidden visually. Every chart has a generated summary: type, series, x range with time zone, y range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and any omissions. `description` adds to the summary and never removes the zone or omission statements. Element ids are unique per chart. | Accessibility-tree snapshot for every fixture; a 24-chart page has no duplicate ids; axe `heading-order` | R11, R13 |\n   138\t| A2 | Keyboard: at most two tab stops while the table is closed, the plot and one toolbar with roving focus that holds legend toggles, zoom, pause and the table control. Every point is reachable in order; the series can be switched; the ends can be reached directly; focus can leave the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px and 3:1 and is not hidden by the tooltip. The exact keys and roles are fixed after the pattern spike in M1 (section 8). | Forward and reverse traversal in collapsed, zoomed, streaming and table-open states; focus after hiding the focused series; indicator contrast | R12 |\n   139\t| A3 | Moving focus to a point conveys its text (series, x, value with unit, position) through the focused element's accessible name or a polite live region. On touch screen readers, every point is reachable by swiping, or the table is reachable within two gestures. | Accessibility-tree snapshot after each key; a recorded rehearsal on NVDA with Chrome, NVDA with Firefox, VoiceOver with Safari on macOS, VoiceOver on iOS, and TalkBack with Chrome | R12, R13 |\n   140\t| A4 | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from the normalized data when opened: the title as caption, field labels with units as column headers, x values as row headers, values formatted as in F6. It covers the current x range, 100 rows per page, with every row reachable by paging or by jumping to an x value. It sits in a focusable scroll region no taller than the plot, and opening it does not move other charts. | Table content compared with fixture data, including a value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000; a 24-chart page layout test | R13 |\n   141\t| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\n   142\t| A6 | Content reflows at 320 CSS px and 400% zoom without two-dimensional scrolling. User text-spacing overrides do not clip text. | Viewport and style-override tests in three engines | WCAG 1.4.10, 1.4.12 |\n   143\t| A7 | Data arriving does not speak. Announcements happen only for the reader's own navigation, and for a change of state (L7). While data streams, keyboard focus stays on the same datum until it leaves the window. | Stream test counting live-region changes with and without focus; 24 concurrent streams | R12 |\n   144\t| A8 | axe-core reports no violations on any fixture in any state, including `heading-order` and `scrollable-region-focusable`. | CI | R11 |\n   145\t| A9 | A conformance table lists every applicable WCAG 2.2 AA success criterion, the test that covers it, and what the author must supply (a meaningful title or `labelledBy`, series names, units, the page's own structure). It also states the limits in section 3.8. | Published with each release; a CI check that every listed test exists | R11 |\n   146\t| A10 | Every built-in string can be replaced through `messages`. Built-in English text is marked `lang=\"en\"` when the document language is not English. | Test on a `lang=\"de\"` page | WCAG 3.1.2 |\n   147\t\n   148\t### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\n   149\t\n   150\tLevel decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the chart shows its error state. **Warning**: something is not drawn as given; the reader sees the note in F12. **Hint**: advice for the author. Errors and warnings print once per code per page in every build; hints print in development builds only. `strict: true` throws on errors and warnings found while validating a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. A diagnostic that can only arise later (a contrast check after the page's CSS changes, a custom formatter that throws when a table page is built, a label that stops fitting after a resize) is reported when it is found and is never thrown. Every diagnostic names the chart; those about data name the field and the first row indexes in the caller's own data.\n   151\t\n   152\t| Code | Level | Condition | What is drawn |\n   153\t|---|---|---|---|\n   154\t| AX100 | Error | Typed columns have different lengths | Error state; the message names the columns and their lengths |\n   155\t| AX101 | Error | A named field is not a key of any row | Error state; the message lists the keys that exist and the closest match |\n   156\t| AX102 | Warning | A field is absent (`undefined`) in some rows | Gaps |\n   157\t| AX103 | Warning | A value is `NaN` or infinite | Gap |\n   158\t| AX104 | Hint | Every non-missing value of a value field is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`) | Parsed and drawn; the message suggests `parse: 'number'`. An empty string counts as missing |\n   159\t| AX105 | Hint | Steps are absent on a regular time, date, wall-clock or number axis | Gaps. When a series would need more absent steps than twice its observations (and more than 1,000), it is treated as irregular instead: no gaps are created and the hint says so |\n   160\t| AX106 | Warning | A value field mixes numbers with strings, or holds strings that are not plain decimals (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`) | The strings are not drawn |\n   161\t| AX107 | Warning | A date or time cannot be parsed | That row is not drawn |\n   162\t| AX108 | Error | Duplicate x within a series, or duplicate donut labels | That series, or the donut, is not drawn |\n   163\t| AX109 | Hint | Numbers on an axis declared `time` look like seconds, or a numeric x looks like epoch milliseconds without `type: 'time'` | As declared |\n   164\t| AX110 | Warning | An author domain on bar or area excluded zero | Domain widened to zero |\n   165\t| AX111 | Hint | Instants look like calendar dates | As instants, zone shown |\n   166\t| AX112 | Hint | Date-time strings have no offset | As written |\n   167\t| AX120 | Error | Negative donut value | Error state |\n   168\t| AX121 | Warning | More than eight donut slices | Seven largest plus \"Other (n)\" |\n   169\t| AX130 | Warning | More than eight series | All series, with dash or marker differences |\n   170\t| AX140 | Warning | No `title` and no `labelledBy` | Generated name |\n   171\t| AX141 | Warning | Palette below 3:1 on the resolved background | As themed |\n   172\t| AX142 | Hint | Plot too small to draw | Summary and table control |\n   173\t| AX143 | Hint | Labels hidden after every remedy | Note shown to the reader |\n   174\t| AX150 | Warning | Unknown option key | Ignored; the message suggests the closest known key |\n   175\t| AX151 | Warning | `append` rows out of order | Accepted through the slower full update |\n   176\t| AX131 | Hint | A compact chart has more than one series | Drawn, with dash differences |\n   177\t| AX132 | Hint | More bars than pixels along the category axis | Drawn; the message suggests aggregating |\n   178\t| AX133 | Hint | A scatter chart has more than 50,000 points | Drawn; the message says drawing may be slow |\n   179\t| AX134 | Error | Series × x positions exceeds 20,000,000 (long rows with many series and little shared x) | Error state; the message gives both counts and suggests fewer series or a scatter chart |\n   180\t| AX152 | Warning | `timeZone` is not a zone the browser knows | The viewer's zone is used |\n   181\t| AX160 | Warning | A custom formatter threw | The default format is used for that value |\n   182\t| AX199 | Error | Axylo itself failed while building the chart | Error state with a generic message; the error is passed to `onDiagnostic` and printed |\n   183\t\n   184\tExpected results for the Phase 1 fixtures: the combined `dirty` fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`) yields AX102, AX103 and AX106 once each, draws Mon–Tue as a segment and Sun as a point, and shows the F12 note. `dirty-string` (one `'40'` among numbers) yields AX106. A column made only of numeric strings yields AX104 and is drawn in full. `dirty-null` yields nothing.\n   185\t\n   186\tThe split between AX104 and AX106 answers a reviewer's objection that common back ends return counts as strings (Postgres `bigint` and `numeric`, BigQuery): a whole column of plain decimal strings is unambiguous and is parsed; a stray string among numbers is a bug and is not.\n   187\t\n   188\t### 3.6 Type matrix\n   189\t\n   190\t| | Line | Area | Bar | Donut | Scatter |\n   191\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   192\t| Picking by pointer | Nearest x | Nearest x | The band under the pointer | The slice under the pointer | Nearest point within 24 px; ties go to the later row |\n   193\t| Tooltip content | Every visible series at that x | Same, plus the stack total | Every series in that category | Label, value, percentage | The point's series, x and y; coincident points listed, up to eight |\n   194\t| Keyboard order | By x, then series | By x, then series | By category, then series | By slice order | By x, then y |\n   195\t| Zoom | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | On time, date and numeric x only | No | Yes, both axes later; x only in 1.0 |\n   196\t| Simplified drawing when dense | F5 | F5 on cumulative sums | No; more bars than pixels raises a hint | No | No in 1.0; above 50,000 points a hint says drawing may be slow |\n   197\t| Limit that raises a diagnostic | Eight series | Eight series | Eight series | Eight slices | Eight series |\n   198\t| Reference lines and bands | Yes; included in the domain; listed in the summary and table caption | Yes | Yes | No | Yes |\n   199\t\n   200\t### 3.7 Compact variant\n   201\t\n   202\t`compact: true` on line, area and bar draws the marks and nothing else: no axes, gridlines, legend, toolbar, table control, zoom or tooltip. It is for a small tile where the page already shows the number and the label.\n   203\t\n   204\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by |\n   205\t|---|---|---|\n   206\t| C1 | A compact chart is a static image with a full text alternative: `role=\"img\"`, an accessible name (as in A1, including AX140 when none is given) and the generated summary as its description (series, x range, minimum, maximum, last value, omissions). It has no tab stop and no pointer-only behavior, so nothing is available to a mouse that is not available to a keyboard. | Accessibility-tree snapshot; a page of 40 has zero added tab stops |\n   207\t| C2 | The fidelity rules still hold: zero baseline for bar and area (F1), gaps (F2), diagnostics (F3), simplified drawing (F5), sorting (F11), no motion (I5), scheme from the page (L4). | The same fixtures as the full charts |\n   208\t| C3 | It fills its container at any size from 24×16 CSS px up; with no definite height it is 32 px tall. | Size tests |\n   209\t| C4 | With more than one series, lines differ by dash as well as color, since there is no legend; AX131 suggests one series per compact chart. | Two-series fixture |\n   210\t| C5 | When values were left out (F12), the description says so and a small marker with a text equivalent appears in the corner. | `dirty` fixture in compact mode |\n   211\t\n   212\tA compact chart does not give access to every value; the summary is its alternative. An author who needs every value reachable uses the full chart.\n   213\t\n   214\t### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\n   215\t\n   216\tAutomated checks establish structure: names, roles, focus order, contrast, text. They do not establish that a screen-reader user finds a chart understandable. The only evidence for that in 1.0 is A3's rehearsal by the developer on five reader and browser pairs, recorded as rehearsal. Axylo is built by one person with no access to study participants, so no claim of usability testing with disabled users is made, and the conformance table says so in those words. Reviewers proposed participant testing as a release gate; it is recorded here as a known limit instead.\n   217\t\n   218\t## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\n   219\t\n   220\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n   221\t|---|---|---|---|\n   222\t| DX1 | The first chart takes ten lines or fewer, including import, by npm and by script tag. No required option besides data and fields. | Two CI tests that render the documented snippet from the packed distribution | D1 |\n   223\t| DX2 | One logical table, three ways to pass it: long rows, wide rows, typed columns. The three forms of each fixture produce the same normalized data and the same diagnostics, with row indexes in the caller's own form. | Equivalence tests | D2 |\n   224\t| DX3 | With typed rows, a misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, wide `y` combined with `series`, and an option that does not apply to the chart type all fail to compile. At run time, for untyped rows, the same mistakes raise AX101, AX106 and AX150. | [docs/api/examples.ts](api/examples.ts) compiles and each marked mistake fails (already checked with TypeScript 7.0.2); runtime tests per code | D5 |\n   225\t| DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\n   226\t| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\n   227\t| DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\n   228\t| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets, set by the owner on 2026-11-18 after measurement: 96 KiB gzipped for everything and 80 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, CSS included. (The first targets, 40 and 25 KiB, were written before anything was built; an analysis of the built library showed they cannot hold the accessibility layer, the table, diagnostics, three kinds of time and streaming: `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size.analysis.md`.) | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\n   229\t| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Creating, destroying and creating again on the same element works and leaks nothing (the sequence a framework's strict or development mode produces). Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\n   230\t| DX9 | Semantic versioning. A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and six months. At most one major version a year. The previous release's documented examples still compile and run against each release candidate. | Release policy; API report diff; upgrade test | D6 |\n   231\t| DX10 | Every runnable example in the documentation is compiled and rendered in CI from the packed distribution. | Docs build | D1 |\n   232\t| DX11 | MIT license for everything, including zoom and accessibility. Third-party notices are verified at release. | Repository; release check | D7 |\n   233\t| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\n   234\t\n   235\t## 5. Performance budgets\n   236\t\n   237\tThese numbers are provisional. They are design targets taken from Phase 1's order-of-magnitude measurements, and they become release gates only after the benchmark harness built in M0 has measured a working Axylo slice in M1 and the limits have been recalibrated and frozen (plan, M1 and M6). Each budget names its workload, metric and statistic so that two people measuring the same build get the same verdict.\n   238\t\n   239\t| ID | Workload | Metric | Statistic | Provisional limit | Phase 1 reference |\n   240\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   241\t| P1 | `trend` (3×120), default options, reference desktop | Main-thread task time from the call until the chart is complete | Median of 20 runs | 10 ms | Not comparable across libraries below about 35 ms with the Phase 1 method |\n   242\t| P2 | One series, 100,000 and 1,000,000 points, typed columns | Same | Median of 20 | 60 ms and 250 ms | See 01, section 5.3: uPlot and tuned Chart.js are the fastest measured |\n   243\t| P3 | 600-point window, one appended point per display frame at 60 Hz, driven by an independent clock | Main-thread task time per update (per run: task time in the window divided by the number of updates); and frames later than 25 ms | Median over runs; count per 300 frames | 2 ms; 0 | See 01, section 5.3 |\n   244\t| P4 | Pointer moved across a 100,000-point chart | Time from input to the frame showing the matching readout | 95th percentile | One frame (16.7 ms at 60 Hz) | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   245\t| P5 | 24 `trend` charts created on one page, each tile 340 × 200 px | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 200 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   246\t| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |\n   247\t| P8 | 40 compact charts created on one page, each 160 × 48 px with one series | Total main-thread task time | Median of 20 | 80 ms | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   248\t| P7 | Memory | Bytes owned by Axylo beyond the caller's columns for 1,000,000 points; heap trend over 1,000 create–destroy cycles | Measured after forced collection | At most 1× the size of the caller's two columns; no upward slope | Not measured in Phase 1 |\n   249\t\n   250\tThree rules hold whatever the numbers become:\n   251\t\n   252\t1. Budgets apply to default options. Finding D8 showed libraries that are fast only for authors who know which options to set.\n   253\t2. Budgets are absolute, on named hardware profiles. Comparison libraries, uPlot above all, are measured in the same runs and published beside Axylo, but a ratio to another library is not a gate: it would reward leaving out work that library does not do.\n   254\t3. The accessibility layer is on in every measured configuration.\n   255\t\n   105\tThere is no `width`, `height`, `responsive`, `theme`, `palette` or `animation` option. Size comes from the container, colors from CSS, motion from the spec (I5). An unknown key raises AX150 with the closest known name.\n   106\t\n   107\t### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\n   108\t\n   109\t```ts\n   110\tchart.update({ title: 'Sessions' });                 // options\n   111\tchart.append([{ day: '2026-05-01', channel: 'Direct', sessions: 41210 }], { keep: 120 });   // rows\n   112\tchart.zoom(['2026-03-01', '2026-03-31']);            // view\n   113\tconst off = chart.on('hover', point => { … });\n   114\tchart.diagnostics;                                   // current, as soon as any call returns\n   115\tchart.destroy();\n   116\t```\n   117\t\n   118\tFinding D3: the ten libraries have seven update idioms. Axylo has three methods, one per kind of change, with one contract.\n   119\t\n   120\t| Rule | Detail |\n   121\t|---|---|\n   122\t| Order | Calls are applied in the order made. `update({ data })` followed by `append` appends to the new data. |\n   123\t| Two phases | Each call validates and normalizes synchronously: diagnostics are current when it returns, and a `strict` chart throws from that call. Drawing is deferred to the next animation frame, and calls made before that frame are drawn once. |\n   124\t| Atomic switch | Marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer move to a new data revision together. No frame mixes two revisions. |\n   125\t| Patches | A key present in an `update` replaces the old value whole; a key set to `undefined` returns to its default; absent keys stay. No deep merging. |\n   126\t| Rejected changes | A call that throws under `strict` leaves the chart on its last valid state. |\n   127\t| Streaming | `keep` is the number of distinct x values retained. Rows before the last x are accepted through the slower full update and raise AX151. While zoomed to the latest edge the view follows new data; zoomed elsewhere, it stays put. Paused charts buffer (I6). Keyboard focus stays on its datum until that datum leaves the window (A7). |\n   128\t| Teardown | `destroy()` cancels the pending frame, disconnects observers and listeners, and makes later calls no-ops. |\n   129\t\n   130\t### 1.6 Diagnostics\n   131\t\n   132\tThe registry, with each code's level and outcome, is in the spec (section 3.5). Example for the Phase 1 combined dirty fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`):\n   133\t\n   134\t```\n   135\t[axylo AX103] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" has 1 value that is not a finite number (NaN at row 3). Shown as a gap.\n   136\t  Check the calculation that produced it, or send null for \"no data\".\n   137\t[axylo AX106] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" mixes numbers with 1 string (\"40\" at row 4). The string is not plotted.\n   138\t  Convert it to a number where the data is produced.\n   139\t[axylo AX102] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" is absent in 1 row (row 5). Shown as a gap.\n   140\t  Send null if the value is known to be missing.\n   141\t```\n   142\t\n   143\tThe reader of that chart sees \"Values not shown: 3. See table.\" (F12).\n   144\t\n   145\t`validate(type, options)` runs the same normalization without a DOM and returns the diagnostics, so a server, a test or a coding agent can check a chart before rendering it.\n   146\t\n   147\tDevelopment and production flavours are selected through package `exports` conditions. The script-tag distribution ships `axylo.js` (development) and `axylo.min.js` (production). Errors and warnings print once per code per page in both; hints only in development.\n   148\t\n   149\t### 1.7 React adapter (not in 1.0)\n   169\t## 2. Architecture\n   170\t\n   171\t### 2.1 Pipeline\n   172\t\n   173\t```\n   174\toptions ─► normalize ─► frame ──┬─► view model ─► layout ─► scene ─► mark renderer\n   175\t            │ validate   (authoritative) │   scales, visible range         text renderer (DOM)\n   176\t            ▼ diagnostics                ├─► lookup: exact values by x, by point, by ordinal\n   177\t                                         ├─► summary and table\n   178\t                                         └─► focus model\n   179\t```\n   180\t\n   181\tThe **frame** is the normalized, lossless data: typed columns, a validity bitmap, dictionary-encoded categories, source-row indexes and a revision number. It is the only source for exact values. The table, the summary, keyboard navigation, tooltips and events read the frame. The **scene** is geometry for drawing and may be simplified (F5); nothing reads values back from it. The first draft derived accessibility from the scene, and two reviewers showed that a simplified drawing cannot return the samples it dropped.\n   182\t\n   183\t| Stage | Output | Notes |\n   184\t|---|---|---|\n   185\t| normalize | Frame, diagnostics | The only place raw input is read. Never coerces silently. |\n   186\t| view model | Scales, visible index ranges, series visibility | Domain rules implement F1 and F7. |\n   187\t| lookup | Nearest-x by binary search; nearest-point by a grid index (scatter) | Works on the frame within the visible range; cost bounded independently of the drawing. |\n   188\t| layout | Plot rectangle, tick sets, label boxes, legend and control placement | Implements L1, L3, L8. Takes a text-measurement interface as input. |\n   189\t| scene | Flat list of primitives (`path`, `rect`, `arc`, `rule`, `text`) | Plain data; testable in Node with injected text metrics. |\n   190\t| renderers | Marks; DOM text; accessibility DOM | See 2.3. |\n   191\t\n   192\t### 2.2 Invalidation\n   193\t\n   194\tStages are cached, and the cache keys are explicit. Caching on object identity alone, as the first draft said, would miss font loads, pixel-ratio changes and in-place mutation.\n   195\t\n   196\t| Stage | Reruns when |\n   197\t|---|---|\n   198\t| normalize | Data revision or field encoding changes. `append` extends the frame without renormalizing earlier rows. |\n   199\t| view model | Frame revision, zoom, series visibility, author domains. |\n   200\t| layout | View model; container size; resolved font and text spacing; `document.fonts` finishing a load; locale; any label text. A data-only update reruns layout when the tick labels would change, and skips it otherwise. |\n   201\t| scene | Layout or view model. |\n   202\t| mark raster | Scene; device pixel ratio; resolved colors (L4's triggers). |\n   203\t\n   204\tText is measured with the page's computed font, letter spacing and word spacing applied to the measuring context, and layout reruns when fonts finish loading. The measurement interface is a parameter, so Node tests inject fixed metrics and browser tests use the real one.\n   205\t\n   206\t### 2.3 Rendering\n   207\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git show bbd7564:docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md | rg -n 'rule|corridor|140|80|coincident|readout|pinned|lock|evic' \ngit show bbd7564:vitest.config.ts\ngit show bbd7564:package.json\ngit diff --name-only bbd7564\ngit ls-files --stage packages/axylo/dist\nnode --version\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git show bbd7564:docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md | rg -n 'rule|corridor|140|80|coincident|readout|pinned|lock|evic' \ngit show bbd7564:vitest.config.ts\ngit show bbd7564:package.json\ngit diff --name-only bbd7564\ngit ls-files --stage packages/axylo/dist\nnode --version\"","aggregated_output":"7:Revision 1 (placed across the reading axis, offset from the pointer, interactive while hovered) was implemented three times and still failed the acceptance tests: because the tooltip's position followed the pointer it moved away when approached, because it took pointer events it stalled the readout or swallowed the click whenever a move landed on it, and it could cover controls and the focus ring. Revision 2 removes the causes instead of tuning distances. Revision 3 (after the final review, `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core-final.interaction.review.md`) replaces revision 2's separate hold rules with one, and adds rules 8 to 10 and the limits in rules 5 and 6.\n9:**Problem.** WCAG 1.4.13 requires content shown on hover to stay when the pointer moves onto it. Spec I1 requires the readout to name the datum at the pointer on every move, the tooltip not to cover the active mark, to stay inside the viewport, and to fall back to a fixed readout when it cannot fit. On a line chart the whole plot picks, so anything that takes pointer events inside the plot blocks picking.\n16:3b. **A rest holds the datum along the way to the content (revision 3).** Rule 3 alone lets the content change whenever the straight path to it crosses positions that pick something else, and the final review found such paths for every placement: a tooltip beside a tall bar, a donut's tooltip in the hole, a scatter point's tooltip, a tooltip above a point in a dense line near the plot's edge, and the strip under a small plot. So one rule covers all of them, replacing the per-placement cases of revision 2 (including the unconditional hold inside a scatter or donut tooltip's rectangle, which is withdrawn): once the pointer has rested on a datum for 250 ms (no move larger than 2 CSS px, measured in viewport pixels), that datum is held while the pointer stays inside the corridor between its resting position and the rectangle of the content shown for it (the floating tooltip, or the strip in readout mode), that is, the convex hull of the point and the rectangle, and inside the rectangle itself. Leaving the corridor resumes picking on that same move. A pointer that has not rested is never held, in any placement, so a sweep never stalls.\n19:4. **Rows are not controls while hovered.** A hovered tooltip is text. Clicking or tapping the plot pins the tooltip for that datum; a pinned tooltip takes pointer events, stays until Escape, a click elsewhere, or its datum leaves the displayed frame, and its rows are buttons of at least 24 × 24 CSS px (WCAG 2.5.8) that select a series. Keyboard focus shows the same tooltip by the same placement; it is never over the focus ring because the box includes the ring.\n20:5. **Readout mode is decided by layout, not by hover.** When the plot, laid out without a strip, would be narrower than 240 CSS px or shorter than 140 CSS px, the figure is in readout mode: a strip of two text lines (two actual line heights of the strip's own font, whatever the page's text size) is reserved under the plot in the layout, and the content that would be in the tooltip is written there as running text (\"x: series value; series value; +N more\"), wrapping to the two lines and ending in \"+N more\" when it does not fit; at least the x and one row are always shown. The strip is part of the figure's flow: it covers nothing and nothing covers it. In readout mode there is no floating tooltip. A pinned datum makes the strip's entries buttons of 24 px height. If reserving the strip would leave the plot under the minimum of spec L8 (80 px high), no strip is reserved and pointing shows no readout at all: the datum is still highlighted, and its values are in the keyboard reading and the table. Such a plot is never put into floating mode.\n21:6. **When no candidate is valid outside readout mode** (a tooltip taller than the room around a tall stack, for example), rows are dropped from the end and counted in \"+N more\" until a candidate is valid. The tooltip is never wider or taller than the visible part of the plot (its content wraps; the visible part is the plot rectangle cut by the viewport and by every clipping ancestor's real clipping edge). If no candidate is valid with one row, the candidate that overlaps the active box least is used, shifted along its free axis as far as needed to avoid every control and every focus ring: controls and focus rings are never covered, in any case. This is the only case in which the active mark may be partly covered, and the table still has every value.\n22:7. Escape dismisses a hovered or pinned tooltip without moving the pointer, until the pointer reaches a different datum. One Escape does one thing: while a tooltip, a pin or a drag is active, the chart consumes the key and nothing else reacts to it (a surrounding dialog stays open, single-series reading stays on); the next Escape goes to whatever is next.\n23:8. **A pin is one datum.** The pin remembers the datum the reader chose. Other data that come to share its position are listed with it while it is displayed; when it leaves the displayed frame or is hidden, the pin ends, whatever else is at that position. A dismissal is remembered for that datum in the same way. With `tooltip: false` nothing can be pinned: a click still selects and takes focus.\n24:9. **Pinned content is real.** A pinned tooltip or strip is exposed to assistive technology with its row buttons named by their text; rows keep their element across redraws while their identity is unchanged, so a press that spans a data update still completes.\n27:Rejected: revision 1; an interactive hovered tooltip with a \"safe triangle\" (still ambiguous when the reading axis and the approach coincide); always using the readout (loses the association between the values and the mark on plots that have room).\n44:To check with a screen reader when one is available (added to decision 0001's list): idle appends with the plot focused are silent; an eviction of the focused point is spoken once; an increment immediately after an append lands on the expected neighbour and reads the current count.\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config';\n\n// Unit and property tests run in Node. Browser tests (*.browser.test.ts) drive system Chrome through\n// Playwright against the built bundle: run `npm run build` first, then `npm run test:browser`.\nexport default defineConfig({\n  define: { __DEV__: 'true' },\n  test: {\n    include: process.env.AXYLO_BROWSER\n      ? ['packages/*/test/**/*.browser.test.ts', 'apps/docs/test/**/*.browser.test.ts']\n      : ['packages/*/test/**/*.test.ts', 'apps/docs/test/**/*.test.ts'],\n    exclude: process.env.AXYLO_BROWSER ? ['**/node_modules/**'] : ['**/node_modules/**', '**/*.browser.test.ts'],\n    // Forked processes with --expose-gc: the store's tests force a collection to check that dropped rows are\n    // released, and fail loudly when they cannot.\n    pool: 'forks',\n    execArgv: ['--expose-gc'],\n    testTimeout: process.env.AXYLO_BROWSER ? 60000 : 10000,\n    fileParallelism: !process.env.AXYLO_BROWSER,\n  },\n});\n{\n  \"name\": \"axylo-workspace\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"workspaces\": [\n    \"packages/*\"\n  ],\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"typecheck\": \"tsc -p packages/axylo --noEmit && tsc -p docs/api && tsc -p tests/dist\",\n    \"test\": \"vitest run\",\n    \"build\": \"node scripts/build.mjs\",\n    \"size\": \"node scripts/size.mjs\",\n    \"docs:build\": \"node apps/docs/build.mjs\",\n    \"docs:screenshots\": \"node apps/docs/screenshots.mjs\",\n    \"test:browser\": \"AXYLO_BROWSER=1 vitest run\",\n    \"test:dist\": \"vitest run --config tests/dist/vitest.config.ts\",\n    \"test:dist:browser\": \"AXYLO_BROWSER=1 vitest run --config tests/dist/vitest.config.ts\",\n    \"check\": \"npm run typecheck && npm test && npm run build && npm run test:browser && npm run test:dist && npm run test:dist:browser && npm run size\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"@types/node\": \"^26.6.6\",\n    \"axe-core\": \"^4.14.1\",\n    \"esbuild\": \"^0.28.2\",\n    \"fast-check\": \"^4.10.2\",\n    \"playwright-core\": \"^1.65.1\",\n    \"typescript\": \"^7.0.2\",\n    \"vitest\": \"^5.1.0\"\n  }\n}\npackages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts\npackages/axylo/src/core/focus.ts\npackages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts\npackages/axylo/test/browser/a11y/util.ts\npackages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a5-not-colour-alone.browser.test.ts\npackages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts\npackages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/util.ts\npackages/axylo/test/browser/harness.ts\nv24.21.0\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
