  * number of lattice points in the pick square, (ceil(49 × ratio) + 1)². Tests pass 0 to force the lattice.\n    45\t   */\n    46\t  readonly exactBudget?: number;\n    47\t}\n    48\t\n    49\t/** Thrown inside an exact pick that ran out of budget; never escapes `pick`. */\n    50\tconst OVER_BUDGET = Symbol('over budget');\n    51\t\n    52\t/**\n    53\t * The pointer index of a scatter view (contract: `Lookup.pick`).\n    54\t *\n    55\t * A pick first compares exact positions. When the points near the pointer make that expensive (all of them\n    56\t * at nearly the same distance, as on a ring around the pointer, or a dense cluster), the pick is redone on a\n    57\t * second index where every point is rounded to the device-pixel lattice and the points of one lattice point\n    58\t * are merged, keeping their count and their latest source row. A lattice pick examines at most the occupied\n    59\t * lattice points near the pointer, so its cost depends on the radius and the pixel ratio, not on the number\n    60\t * of points; a rounded position is off by at most 0.71 device px, and the datum's values stay exact.\n    61\t */\n    62\texport function scatterGrid(\n    63\t  view: ScatterView, layout: PlotLayout & { projection: Extract<PlotLayout['projection'], { kind: 'xy' }> },\n    64\t  count: number, devicePixelRatio: number, options: GridOptions = {},\n    65\t) {\n    66\t  const { plot, projection } = layout;\n    67\t  const columns = Math.floor(plot.width / CELL) + 1;\n    68\t  const rows = Math.floor(plot.height / CELL) + 1;\n    69\t  const series = new Uint32Array(count);\n    70\t  const points = new Uint32Array(count);\n    71\t  const xs = new Float64Array(count);\n    72\t  const ys = new Float64Array(count);\n    73\t  const ratio = Number.isFinite(devicePixelRatio) && devicePixelRatio > 0 ? devicePixelRatio : 1;\n    74\t  const tieDistance = 0.5 / ratio;\n    75\t  const budget = options.exactBudget ?? (Math.ceil(49 * ratio) + 1) ** 2;\n